emale plant is going to have  tissue sampled and sent off for  cannabinoid analysis terpene  anthocyanins phenolics  we're taking a sub-sample of seed for  fatty acid analysis  we'll be chop  we're trying to chop every single plant  out of this field at the end of the  season  and send to our fiber collaborator  crystal home  at the new orleans  fiber  usda fiber lab and he's going to be  evaluating these every plant for many  different  fiber characteristics so we'll get all  these huge data sets and attach them to  each accession  so a plant breeder could say like i want  a  cbg  dominant cultivar with low apical  dominance that has  low pectin i whatever you can think of  like you can you can find germplasm that  fits the set of criteria that you're  looking for so that's just a little bit  of what we're doing i'm going to pass it  off to our hemp breeder tyler gordon  as well by the way if you want to look  in the field you can scan the qr code on  the left  and that will link you to grin global  which is a online page for each one of  these accessions  it's kind of dip operate of data right  now but  by end of season we should have a lot  more  info there  is that okay  all right  thank you all for attending this  afternoon it looks like a terrific crowd  out there and we've heard some  fantastic  research studies that have been ongoing  this morning and  so really excited like zach to share a  little bit more about the usda  ars collection  my name is is tyler gordon and again i'm  with usda ars i came  just recently a few months ago from the  usda ars lab in idaho and there i worked  on small grains so the grain part of  hemp i'm very familiar with the  secondary metabolite profiles and fiber  not not too familiar with yet however as  zach mentioned we're going to be doing  lots of evaluations this year  i think we're going to take over 100  individual trait  data points from each one of these  plants here and that's going to be super  informative for breeders  regardless of what the in quality end  product is that you're breeding for  so whether that's fiber or or grain  secondary metabolites or or maybe a  combination of some of those things i  think that'll be really useful data  that'll be posted on the usda  grin global website so anyone  throughout the world has access to that  information  additionally i think zach mentioned this  the seeds from this collection are going  to be available to  re the research community  the one stipulation that we have from  these seeds is that they  in order for us to distribute them  they're going to have to be low thc so  if you're looking for those high thc  cultivars or varieties or accessions  you're not going to be able to get them  from us  one  thing that i would ask of you guys so  we're just kind of starting this this  pre-breeding program here  i'm hoping that you have access to the  qr codes i want to hand around our flyer  here  and if you would scan the qr code  there's a survey there and i'd like  everybody to kind of if if you're  interested in hemp material for your  breeding programs if you could scan the  qr code fill out the survey it's very  quick i think there's what 10 questions  or something and that can kind of help  us  determine what kind of breeding  priorities  we should set  for this material so we'll have all this  terrific data from this from this  location we also have a couple other  locations that we're going to be  collaborating with on the west coast so  oregon state washington state also  hoping to get developed collaborations  with some groups in the midwest and also  in the south to see how these plants  perform  across geographical locations i think  some of the other folks today have have  talked about the importance of  some genotypes might look terrific here  in geneva but they wouldn't necessarily  look  fabulous say in louisiana or alabama  and then one other thing i want to talk  about just a little bit is  some of the disease and insect  resistance that we heard about this  morning  that's i think one of the  really amazing things about these  collections  is  how they can harp they can act as a  reservoir for important um genetic  diversity and also important genes that  we might not even know we need  for the crop at this point  and so for instance  if there's a new disease that we're not  familiar with and it comes in 10 years  that's what these germplasm collections  are are here for is  you might have like a really ratty  looking  you can see some of this material out  here where  it's a land race or a ditch weed from  nebraska and it looks like it has no  value whatsoever but you screen that  against some new insect or disease and  you can find some really interesting  resistance there  and so again all this data is going to  be made  publicly available the germplasm is also  going to be available to the hemp  community  please fill out this the survey the  online survey that's really going to  help us  oh yeah yeah  and just  one other point i wanted to thank the  our field crew field team um zach and i  did not do all this work ourselves and  so i see some folks out here nick  dan  tony i saw peter earlier and so thank  you so much and i also wanted to thank  the organizers cornell for letting us  highlight this um terrific diversity in  in hip um and so if you have any  questions please let me know also please  feel free to walk through the material  as zach said where is that oh you have  the sign again at the start of each plot  is one of these signs  there's a qr code that will link you up  with grin  which is our online the usda online  genetic  database on the front side it gives you  some more information like the field  number the backside has the cultivar if  that's maybe more familiar with people  and so i can take questions at this  point or you can ask them at any point  the signs are on the north end of the  plot they're serpentine serpentine  so you're  you're gonna have to kind of look for on  it yeah  so let's say uh  right you're sending all these samples  to be uh tested out  we're measuring 100 different variables  of the phenotype but the expression of  the phenotype depends on a lot of  factors right so how  do you average over many seasons are  there's many sites or how does that work  that that's a really great question so  green global which is like tyler  mentioned our online database is  um really tooling up to capture these  robust high dimensional data sets so  kind of the old model was you had one  trade for that the tomatoes are yellow  the new grand model is such that we can  attach  large data sets that can capture  multi-year multi-environment trials  and keep those attached to not only the  accession but the the inventory the seed  lot within that accession  and then also  um  as these collections are genotyped those  markers will also be attached to the  accession as well  great question  come on there's got to be a few more  questions in here  yes go for it well then for the  similar for the genotype let's say  you're preserving uh  well you're preserving varieties  cultivars here  but if the if if the  cultivar originates from a different  environment does that is there a worry  or how do you deal with potentially the  genotype drifting or adapting to the  local environment yeah so that's the  the classic question in crop genetic  resource conservation right so  there are many different factors um  to maintain that allelic diversity the  heterozygosity within a population  um first of all we don't even really  know how heterozygous a lot of these  materials are i would guess  a lot there's a lot um but that probably  varies from cultivar to culture so  um in a perfect world you would maintain  an infinite population size  and store  a zillion seeds from that random mating  you can't do that with our seed bank  size and capacity and it's a few of us  so how do you find the sweet spot of  optimal population size  pollination control  running regens from older seed lots  there's a lot of best practices that you  can take  to maintain that genetic diversity long  term  you didn't ask this question but  our seed lots are also split up and  stored in multiple locations in the  event of something catastrophic  happening so we have a mothership  gene bank at uh fort collins colorado  that we'll send aliquots of the seeds to  and then those seeds in turn will get  backed up at this far bald seed vault in  norway and uh and according to a black  box  so  um that's something that's very much on  our mind and as hemp has not been  uh  conserved  in a rigorous scientific way  for the most part there's a lot we're  trying to learn so i'm pointing out some  of the work of nick jenna my postdoc  here so he's looking at how do you store  pollen long term  um it's there's just a lot of open  standing questions that we're trying to  drill into  that will you know provide a cohesive  set of principles  to maintain that genetic diversity long  term  and i just want to add really quickly  you've probably heard from some of the  other groups today about the  heterogeneity levels within things that  are even classified as cultivars and so  pay close attention as you move through  the field today  look you can see a label  and then there will be 10 plants behind  that level label and you'll see they  don't  all look alike or even close to like in  some cases sometimes within one cultivar  you'll have some plants that are five  feet tall and some that are less than a  foot tall and so that's something we  that's one of the priorities for the  breeding program is is to release stuff  that has a lot  more uniformity within the cultivar  great thanks very much tyler and zach  we are  very very fortunate to have uh the  hampton plaza repository here at cornell  agritech mpgru we thank senator schumer  for that  his office uh made the initial push  for that uh  location and we really appreciate that  because it's great to work with these  folks and we have immediate access to  their knowledge and resources  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

